[INFO] 09-22 09:18:42 run_experiments.py:98] **Core Principle Identification**: The problem is asking us to determine the conditions under which a certain sequence of states of a line of lamps will eventually reach a state where all lamps are off. The key principle to apply here is the concept of a "domino effect" or a chain reaction. Given the rule of the lamps' state change, if a lamp is off and its neighbor is on, it will be on in the next minute. Conversely, if a lamp is on and its neighbor is off, it will be off in the next minute. Therefore, for all lamps to be off, there must be no "on" lamps "infecting" their neighbors.
[INFO] 09-22 09:18:42 run_experiments.py:98] **Pitfall Prediction**: A common pitfall is to overlook the boundary conditions, where lamps have only one neighbor. Another trap is assuming the sequence will always reach a stable state where all lamps are off without considering the initial configuration of the lamps.
[INFO] 09-22 09:18:42 run_experiments.py:98] **Strategy Formulation**: 1. Identify the initial state of the lamps. Determine which lamps are on and which are off. 2. Apply the rule of state change for each minute, analyzing the effect of each lamp's state on its neighbors. 3. Determine the maximum possible chain reaction. 4. If the maximum chain reaction reaches the last lamp, all lamps will be off. Otherwise, there is no guarantee that all lamps will be off.
[INFO] 09-22 09:18:42 run_experiments.py:98] </think>
